\begin_layout Standard
While the widely used token level routing within mixture of experts MoE
 has been shown to be an effective way to scale up large languge models,
 while keeping inference costs down .
 The MoE architecture itself could hold interesting properties for interpretabil
ity if expert specialisation could be domain specific, rather than based
 on token properties, letting us see in real time what experts are used
 to answer different types of prompts based on routing behaviour.
 However this types of analysis becomes useful to the extent that experts
 actaully becomes topic experts.
 Projects like Steer Moe () show that analysis of routing patterns and dynamic
 altering of them in regards to alignement can be an effective way of chaning
 model behaviour during inference, strengtening the argument around why
 expert domain interpretability might proive useful properties for this
 type of model behaviour adjustments.
 Clear domain expert specialisation could also show intersting properties
 in terms of model modularity and for mitigating catastrophic forgetting
 (Demix).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
...
 et al (demix) also show great potential for hard routing based on document
 content.
 However this approach heavily relies on labelled domain specfic data, which
 is a major limitation for LLMs often trained on large unlabelled datasets
 such as openWebText.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fan et al shows that sequence level routing orgianically gives rise to a
 weak form of this human like domain expertise, but not to an extent that
 would make this type of routing behaviour intersting for an alignment point
 of view or as strong as ..et al (Demix).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We propose a middle ground where pretraning or weight upcycling of narrowly
 trained domain experts are combined in the MoE and then continously trained
 on unstructured data.
 Our hypothesis is that initilisation with pretrained weights may strenghten
 the weak organic domain specilisation discovered by fan et al and hence
 provide a step towards clearer more interpretable and scalable domain specilisa
tion in MoE architectures.
\end_layout

